WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.170
Deliver on time, get paid, and know your real hourly rate.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:08.570
This is the video for module three of Freelancing Fundamentals.

3
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:17.860
Clients come back to freelancers who are reliable, not only talented. Reliable means three things. You deliver what was agreed.

4
00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:20.540
You deliver when you said.

5
00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:24.520
And the client never has to chase you for news.

6
00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:30.660
Then that good work must become money in your account, and you must check that it really pays.

7
00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:39.760
Stage one, kick-off. Confirm the scope, collect every material the client promised, and say when the first draft will arrive.

8
00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:53.660
Stage two, plan backwards. For example, Zainab needs 15 hours for 12 designs. She adds about 20 percent buffer, making 18 hours, and plans to finish on day 6 of 7.

9
00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:58.020
When a power cut cost her 3 hours, she still delivered a day early.

10
00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:09.640
Stage three, draft and self-check. Tick every item in the scope. Errors in names, prices and phone numbers are the ones clients notice first and forgive least.

11
00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:20.490
Stage four, revisions. Ask the client to put all changes in one message per round. Scattered voice notes are the fastest way to miss a change.

12
00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:28.740
Stage five, handover. Name files clearly, send the format the client needs, and check the share link opens.

13
00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:39.290
If something will be late, say so as soon as you know, not on the deadline day. Clients forgive an early warning far more easily than a silent delay.

14
00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:49.540
An invoice is a formal request for payment. Send one for every job, even for friends, and number them in order so you can find them later.

15
00:01:50.790 --> 00:02:01.760
Zainab lists each piece of work: 60,000 for the posts, 15,000 for the extra posts and 12,000 for the flyer. That is 87,000 naira.

16
00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:07.360
Then she takes off the deposit she already received, 30,000 naira.

17
00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:16.400
The balance due is 57,000 naira. The client sees exactly how it was reached, so there is nothing to argue about.

18
00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:28.720
Most late payments are disorganisation, not bad faith. Stay calm, keep each reminder short, and attach the invoice again. On the due date, send a friendly reminder.

19
00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:33.640
At 3 days late, ask if there is any problem with the invoice.

20
00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:41.670
At 7 days late, a firmer note. Further work and final files are on hold until payment, as agreed.

21
00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:46.820
After that, call. For large sums, ask a trusted adviser.

22
00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:56.250
This is why the deposit matters. And if you met the client on a platform, keep payment on the platform, or you lose its protection.

23
00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:07.290
Being busy and being profitable are not the same. Your effective hourly rate is money received divided by all your working hours, paid and unpaid.

24
00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.370
Amaka received 180,000 naira.

25
00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:17.380
She spent 60 billable hours on those jobs, so 3,000 naira an hour.

26
00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:28.080
But she also spent 30 hours on outreach, calls and admin. Over 90 hours, she earned only 2,000 naira an hour, less than half her minimum.

27
00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:38.590
Her tracker showed why: unplanned revisions, and 12 hours on free samples. So she now limits revisions to 2 rounds and free samples to 30 minutes.

28
00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:47.260
To remember. Plan backwards with a buffer. Check your work against the scope. Send an itemised invoice for every job.

29
00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:53.140
Chase late payment calmly. And review your numbers each month, testing one change at a time.

30
00:03:54.390 --> 00:04:01.730
Now plan one job end to end, set up your invoice template and records folder, and build your tracker in Google Sheets.

31
00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.020
Continue with the lessons below.
